{
  "dom_id": "step-q127693",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Consider the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t-3t\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+3t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=\\frac{9}{2}{t}^{2}.$ Given that $R(s)$ is the parametrization of the given curve by its arc length $s,$ what is the $y$-component of $R?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, since $s=\\frac{9}{2}{t}^{2},$ we have that $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2s}}{3}.$\n\nThe vector function that pametrizes the curve $r(t)$ by its arc length $s,$ with $s\\ge 0,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{\\sqrt{2s}}{3}) \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2s}}{3})-3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2s}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2s}}{3}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2s}}{3})+3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2s}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2s}}{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2s})-\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2s}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2s})+\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2s})⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the $y$-component of $R$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2s})+\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2s}).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [
